<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siege of Kawagoe Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1545">1545</a>–<a href="/years/1546">1546</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Kawagoe, Night Battle of Kawagoe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siege of Kawagoe Castle was a battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a> in which the Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-clan">Hojo clan</a> defended Kawagoe Castle in Musashi province against a large coalition of the Uesugi clans and the Ashikaga of Koga. In 1546 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-ujiyasu">Hojo Ujiyasu</a> relieved the castle with a surprise night attack that routed the besiegers. The victory made the Hojo the leading power in the Kanto.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siege-of-kawagoe-castle.jpg" alt="Siege of Kawagoe Castle" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ocdp · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kawagoejou.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Uesugi Tomosada, eldest legitimate son of Uesugi Tomooki of the Ōgigayatsu branch of the Uesugi clan, was still a child when his father died in 1537, yet he led an attack on the Later Hōjō in the Tachibana district of Musashi province and fortified the temple of Kandai-ji as a castle. Soon afterwards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-ujitsuna">Hōjō Ujitsuna</a> took Kawagoe Castle from Tomosada's uncle, Uesugi Tomonari.</p><p>In 1545 Tomosada set out to win the castle back. He was joined by his more powerful relative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-norimasa">Uesugi Norimasa</a> of the Yamanouchi branch, who held the post of Kantō Kanrei, the shōgun's deputy in the Kantō, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-haruuji">Ashikaga Haruuji</a>, the Kantō kubō at Koga, and by many other daimyō of the region opposed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-ujiyasu">Hōjō Ujiyasu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The besieging force numbered around 80,000, yet the garrison of 3,000 under Hōjō Tsunashige held out until April 1546, when his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-ujiyasu">Hōjō Ujiyasu</a> arrived with a relief force of only 8,000. Ujiyasu sent warriors through the siege lines to tell the garrison he had come, and ninja spies reported that the besiegers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-haruuji">Ashikaga Haruuji</a> above all, had grown careless in their confidence of victory. The main fighting took place at Tōmyō-ji.</p><p>The Hōjō risked a night attack by the garrison and the relief force together. Against battlefield custom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> were ordered to leave their heavy armour behind, so as to move fast and unseen, and not to take the heads of the enemies they killed, which denied them the usual record of their deeds. The attack succeeded and Uesugi Tomosada was killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The victory was the decisive turning point in the struggle for the Kantō. With Tomosada's death the Ōgigayatsu branch came to an end, and the defeat brought the Uesugi family close to extinction. The Hōjō attack has been called one of the most notable examples of night fighting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siege of Kawagoe Castle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hojo Ujiyasu won a celebrated victory at <b>Kawagoe</b> in 1546 and expanded across the Kanto, resisting attacks by Uesugi Kenshin and Takeda Shingen.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-hojo-clan-domain">Later Hojo clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
